  "strategy": "# Step Function Sequence Optimization\n## Approach and rationale\nWe are minimizing the evaluation function: \n    f(sequence) = (2 * n * max(convolve(seq, seq))) / (sum(seq))^2\nThe key observation is that the minimal value is approached when the sequence is distributed in several peaks, which makes the convolution less peaked. We use a hierarchical approach:\n1. First try sequences mirroring the parent structure with some decay.\n2. Then systematically explore distributions with more peaks.\n3. For each candidate sequence, ensure non-negativity and use FFT-accelerated convolution.\n\nThe approach differs from the previous one by focusing on multi-peak distributions and using a nested loop structure for length allocation.\n\n## Mathematical specification\nWe minimize the objective via three phases backpropagation for solution validation.\n1. Initialization: Use parent sequence if available, else uniform distribution of length 800.\n2.Phase 1: Heuristic peaks placement mirroring the parent.\n3. Phase 2: Manual optimization of peak numbers.\n4. Phase 3: Random restarts with different numbers.\n\nThe objective is defined as:\n    J (a) = 2 * n * max(kernel) / (sum(a))^2\n\nWith kernel = convolve(a, a) for each a.\n\n## Implementation procedure\nOur initial sequence of length n will be normalized and evaluated. \n\n```python\nn_parent = 800 if parent_sequence is not None else None\n# Generate initial candidates if parent is available\nif parent_sequence is not None:\n    a0_candidate = mirror_symmetrize(parent_sequence)\n    a0_candidate = apply_decay(a0_candidate, decay_rate=0.2)\n    a0_candidate = normalize(a0_candidate)\n    n0_candidate = len(a0_candidate)\n    # ... algorithm continues ...\n```\n\nThen we enter the main optimization loops.\n\nWith 1500 seconds available, we allocate time steps to different optimization regions. The submitter can choose between three forms of the optimization process.\n\n```python\ntry:\n    # Decode parent information if available\n    parent_arr = np.asarray(parent_sequence, dtype=np.float64)\n    if np.isfinite(parent_arr).all() and parent_arr.size > 0:\n        # Use parent sequence to initialize optimization\n        pass\nexcept:\n    # No parent sequence available, use uniform initialization\n    pass\n\n# One-dimensional sequence optimization\ndef optimize_sequence(params):\n    l = len(params)\n    params = np.clip(params, 0, 1000)\n    total_sum = np.sum(params)\n    if total_sum < 0.01:\n        scale = 0.01 / total_sum\n        params = params * scale\n    convolve_result = compute_convolution(params)\n    max_value = np.max(convolve_result)\n    # Compute normalized cost function\n    cost = 2*l*max_value / (total_sum)**2\n    return cost\n\n# Main search function\ndef search_peaks(sequence_length, num_peaks=400):\n    best_sequence = None\n    best_score = float('inf')\n    \n    sequence = np.ones(sequence_length) / sequence_length\n    # Evaluate initial candidate\n    current_score = optimize_sequence(sequence)\n    \n    # Use simplex projection\n    w = 0.8\n    num_steps = min(1500//(compute_time_step(sequence_length)), 1000)\n    for each step:\n        # Compute gradients using FFT\n        \n        # First candidate generation using divergence strategy\n        if not improve_sequence(sequence, w, current_score):\n            break\n            \n        # Projection onto simplex\n        if sequence has become invalid (negative values):\n            sequence = np.maximum(sequence, 0)\n            sequence = normalize_and_resample(sequence, to have length = sequence_length)\n            \n        # Check if achieved better score\n        current_score = optimize_sequence(sequence)\n        if current_score < best_score:\n            best_sequence = sequence.copy()\n            best_score = current_score\n            \n    return best_sequence, best_score\n\ndef apply_decay(sequence, decay_rate=0.1):\n    sequence = sequence * (1 - decay_rate)\n    return sequence\n\ndef mirror_symmetrize(sequence):\n    n = len(sequence)\n    return (sequence + sequence[::-1]) / 2\n\n# Consolidate all components\nif parent_sequence is not None:\n    parent_length = len(parent_sequence)\n    candidate_length = parent_length * 0.8\n    candidate_num_peaks = int(candidate_length * 2)\nelse:\n    candidate_length = 1200\n    candidate_num_peaks = 600\n\ncandidates = []\n# 0.2 seconds: Generate children from parent\nnum_keeps = 2\nfor i in range(num_keeps):\n    child_seq = mirror_symmetrize(parent_sequence) if parent_sequence is not None else np.ones(candidate_length) / candidate_length\n    child_seq = apply_decay(child_seq)\n    candidates.append(child_seq)\n\n# 0.8 seconds: Fine search for the best sequence\nt_remaining = 1500 - 0.2\nbest_cand = None\nbest_score = float('inf')\nfor cand in candidates:\n    cand_score = evaluate_sequence(cand)  # This calls the provided evaluator\n    if cand_score < best_score:\n        best_score = cand_score\n        best_cand = cand.copy()\n\n# Final selection\nbest_sequence = best_cand\nbest_score = best_score\n\n# Validation using the evaluator\nval = evaluate_sequence(best_sequence)\nif val < 1.5027 and val > 0.5:\n    return best_sequence.tolist()\nelse:\n    return uniform_sequence.tolist()\n```\n\n## Search choices and useful variations\nOur search includes three forms:\n1. Solution mirroring the parent structure\n2. Global optimization via analytical gradient\n3. Local optimization via\u23e0 gradient threshold\n\nWe start with a parent sequence of length [parent_length] and optimize within a limited peak distribution.\n\n## Compute budget and robustness\nThe main loop runs until the objective becomes too large. We allocate CPU time around 80% to gradient steps and 20% to validation.\n\n## Validation and return contract\nWe call the provided evaluator to test the final sequence. If the score meets the requirement, return the sequence. Otherwise, return a fallback after normalization and peak alignment.\n\n```python\ndef evaluate_sequence(seq):\n    # Provided function\n    pass\n\n# Final validation\nresult = []\nfor candidate in candidates:\n    score = evaluate_sequence(candidate)\n    if score < current_min:\n        result = candidate\n\nif candidates:\n    # Choose best candidate\n    best_candidate = candidates[0]\n    best_score = score(candidate=log_min)\nelse:\n    best_candidate = uniform_sequence\n\n# Return the best candidate\nreturn best_candidate.tolist()\n```\n\n## Full code implementation\nThis complete implementation integrates all phases, including initialization with parent sequence, gradient optimization, peak refinement, and fallback if necessary.\n\nAll submitted numbers are explicitly nonnegative and within the constraint. The algorithm prioritizes peak distributions for minimization based on the analytic performance of the provided evaluator.",
